WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:04.850
Electrical systems start a great many boat fires, so a few rules.

2
00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:10.350
Batteries give off hydrogen gas while they charge, and hydrogen is explosive.

3
00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:13.650
So battery compartments need ventilation.

4
00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:19.375
The batteries must be secured so they can’t shift in a seaway, with the terminals covered.

5
00:00:19.625 --> 00:00:23.275
Use marine-rated wiring, breakers and fuses.

6
00:00:23.525 --> 00:00:26.300
Household parts corrode and overheat.

7
00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:29.525
Check your shore power cords and connectors.

8
00:00:29.775 --> 00:00:34.725
Brown or melted plastic means heat, and heat means a fire on its way.

9
00:00:34.975 --> 00:00:40.050
And one danger most people have never heard of: electric shock drowning.

10
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:46.950
Faulty wiring can put current into the water around a dock, especially in fresh or brackish water.

11
00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:49.875
A swimmer is paralyzed and drowns.

12
00:00:50.125 --> 00:00:53.275
So what’s the rule at any marina with shore power?

13
00:00:54.275 --> 00:00:56.200
Nobody swims there.
